{
  "number": 5,
  "title": "Harmony and Voice Leading II: Chord Progressions and Predominant Function",
  "guide": {
    "summary": "Unit 5 expands harmonic vocabulary: predominant chords and their voice leading, root motion by fifths, six-four chord types, other diatonic progressions, and harmonic rhythm.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Progressions and root motion",
        "body": "Strong progressions often move by descending fifths (vi–ii–V–I) or descending thirds (I–vi–IV–ii). Predominant chords (IV, ii, ii6, ii7, ii6/5) lead to dominant (V, V7, vii°6). ii6 is especially common because its bass, scale degree 4, moves up by step to 5. Retrogressions (e.g., V–IV) are generally avoided in common-practice style. The iii chord is rare and often appears in descending-bass patterns. Harmonic rhythm is the rate at which chords change; it often speeds up approaching a cadence."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Six-four chords and other expansions",
        "body": "Second-inversion triads appear in specific patterns: the cadential 6/4 (I6/4–V, the 6 and 4 resolving down to 5 and 3), the passing 6/4 (bass moves stepwise, e.g., I – V6/4 – I6 or IV – I6/4 – IV6), the pedal or neighbor 6/4 (I – IV6/4 – I over a sustained bass), and the arpeggiated 6/4 (bass arpeggiates a sustained harmony). Tonic expansion often uses V6/4, V4/3, or vii°6 as passing chords between I and I6. In minor keys, raise the leading tone in V and vii°."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Harmonizing a melody",
        "body": "To harmonize a melody or compose a bass line, first identify the key and cadence points, then choose chords that contain each melody note and follow the phrase model (T–PD–D–T). Aim for contrary motion between soprano and bass, avoid parallel perfect intervals between outer voices, and end with an appropriate cadence. Use inversions (I6, ii6, V6) to create a smoother, more melodic bass."
      }
    ],
    "keyTakeaways": [
      "Descending fifths: vi–ii–V–I.",
      "ii6 is a favorite predominant.",
      "Four types of 6/4: cadential, passing, pedal, arpeggiated.",
      "Avoid retrogression V–IV.",
      "Harmonic rhythm accelerates toward cadences."
    ],
    "commonMistakes": [
      "Using a 6/4 chord outside standard patterns.",
      "Parallel octaves between outer voices when harmonizing.",
      "Forgetting the raised leading tone in minor.",
      "Choosing chords that don't contain the melody note."
    ],
    "examTips": [
      "Plan the cadence first when harmonizing.",
      "Label 6/4 types by bass motion.",
      "Use inversions for stepwise bass lines."
    ],
    "cramSheet": "**Root motion:** desc. 5ths (vi ii V I) · desc. 3rds\n\n**PD:** IV · ii · ii6 · ii7 · ii6/5 → V\n\n**6/4 types:** cadential (I6/4–V) · passing (I–V6/4–I6) · pedal (I–IV6/4–I) · arpeggiated\n\n**Tonic expansion:** I – V4/3 (or vii°6) – I6"
  },
